# A183 — the capture run's Caddyfile, and which interface it binds (2026-09-15T15:50:20Z)

## 1. before: the repository's host-less site address, copied verbatim
$ git show HEAD:tools/capture-devices.py | grep -n "replace(\"http://:7788\"" -B 4 -A 2
  186-    config = run_directory() / "Caddyfile.capture"
  187-    config.write_text(
  188-        (ROOT / "Caddyfile")
  189-        .read_text()
  190:        .replace("http://:7788", f"http://:{PORT}")
  191-        .replace("127.0.0.1:7789", f"127.0.0.1:{ENGINE_PORT}")
  192-    )
$ git show HEAD:Caddyfile | grep -n "To bind this Mac only" -A 4
  36:# To bind this Mac only, run `tools/start.sh --local-only`. It generates a copy of this file
  37-# with `bind 127.0.0.1` inserted inside the site block below, which is the directive that
  38-# actually picks the listening interface — a site address of `127.0.0.1:7788` would not, it
  39-# would still listen on ":7788". See "Using the website" in the wiki.
  40-http://:7788 {

## 2. what Caddy actually derives, which is the measurement start.sh's own comment names
$ caddy adapt --config <before> --adapter caddyfile   # the generated config without bind
  listen: [':7791']  <- every interface
$ caddy adapt --config <after> --adapter caddyfile    # the fix
  listen: ['127.0.0.1:7791']  <- this Mac only

## 3. the fix, and the tools that check it
$ grep -n "bind 127.0.0.1" tools/capture-devices.py
  202:    return text.replace(f"http://:{PORT} {{", f"http://:{PORT} {{\n\tbind 127.0.0.1", 1)
$ python3 -c "... capture_caddyfile() ..." | grep bind
  http://:7791 {
  	bind 127.0.0.1
$ pyright tools/capture-devices.py
  0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 informations
$ ruff check tools/capture-devices.py && ruff format --check tools/capture-devices.py
  All checks passed!
  1 file already formatted

  Limit, stated: the capture run itself is not executed — it drives a real browser over the
  Chrome DevTools Protocol — so what is proven is the config it will hand to Caddy, which is the
  thing that decides the interface, and that proof is Caddy's own adapter rather than a regex.
